## Solution

The transformation $T(x,y) = \left(\dfrac{x}{x^2+y^2},\,\dfrac{-y}{x^2+y^2}\right)$ is inversion in the unit circle composed with reflection across the $x$-axis. The square has vertices $(\pm1,\pm1)$ and four edges. I parametrize each edge and find its image.

**Right edge** ($x=1,\; y=t,\; t\in[-1,1]$): With $r^2=1+t^2$,
$$T=(u,v)=\left(\tfrac{1}{1+t^2},\,\tfrac{-t}{1+t^2}\right).$$
Then $u^2+v^2 = \tfrac{1}{1+t^2}=u$, so $\left(u-\tfrac12\right)^2+v^2=\tfrac14$ — a circle centered $\left(\tfrac12,0\right)$, radius $\tfrac12$. As $t:-1\to1$, the point runs from $\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right)$ through $(1,0)$ to $\left(\tfrac12,-\tfrac12\right)$, tracing the **right** semicircle.

**Left edge** ($x=-1,\; y=t$): $T=\left(\tfrac{-1}{1+t^2},\,\tfrac{-t}{1+t^2}\right)$, giving $\left(u+\tfrac12\right)^2+v^2=\tfrac14$ — circle centered $\left(-\tfrac12,0\right)$, radius $\tfrac12$; the **left** semicircle from $\left(-\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right)$ through $(-1,0)$ to $\left(-\tfrac12,-\tfrac12\right)$.

**Top edge** ($y=1,\; x=t$): $T=\left(\tfrac{t}{1+t^2},\,\tfrac{-1}{1+t^2}\right)$, giving $u^2+\left(v+\tfrac12\right)^2=\tfrac14$ — circle centered $\left(0,-\tfrac12\right)$, radius $\tfrac12$; the **bottom** semicircle from $\left(-\tfrac12,-\tfrac12\right)$ through $(0,-1)$ to $\left(\tfrac12,-\tfrac12\right)$.

**Bottom edge** ($y=-1,\; x=t$): $T=\left(\tfrac{t}{1+t^2},\,\tfrac{1}{1+t^2}\right)$, giving $u^2+\left(v-\tfrac12\right)^2=\tfrac14$ — circle centered $\left(0,\tfrac12\right)$, radius $\tfrac12$; the **top** semicircle from $\left(-\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right)$ through $(0,1)$ to $\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right)$.

**The image curve.** The four semicircular arcs join consecutively at the four corner points $\left(\pm\tfrac12,\pm\tfrac12\right)$, forming a single closed curve that bulges outward to $(1,0)$, $(0,-1)$, $(-1,0)$, $(0,1)$.

**Area computation.** The four junction points $\left(\pm\tfrac12,\pm\tfrac12\right)$ are the vertices of an axis-aligned square of side $1$, hence area $1$. Each side of this square is a chord of one of the semicircles. Since each semicircle has radius $\tfrac12$, its diameter is $1$, which is exactly the length of the corresponding side — so **each side of the inner square is a diameter of its semicircle**, and each semicircular bulge lies entirely outside the square. The enclosed region is therefore the disjoint union of the central square and four semicircular regions:

$$\text{Area} = \underbrace{1}_{\text{square}} + 4\cdot\underbrace{\tfrac12\pi\left(\tfrac12\right)^2}_{\text{semicircle}} = 1 + 4\cdot\frac{\pi}{8} = 1 + \frac{\pi}{2}.$$

